alex_h: Heads up — hit a weird ESM/CJS entrypoint issue deploying the Edge SDK to Cloudflare Workers. Build works locally but the worker logs `ReferenceError: exports is not defined` on startup. Anyone seen this?

maria_dev: hmm, that usually means a CJS bundle landed in an environment that expects ESM. What bundler + target did you use?

alex_h: esbuild bundle, platform: browser, target: chrome90, output: dist/index.js. package.json has \"main\": \"dist/index.js\" and \"type\": \"module\". But the SDK package we're importing still seems to have `module.exports` inside.

kevin-runtime: Cloudflare Workers are strict module workers. If something emits `module.exports` it'll blow up. Two quick checks: 1) open the built file and search for `module.exports` 2) ensure the SDK provides an explicit ESM entry (exports.import) or a browser-friendly build.

sophie-typed: Typings annoyance too — TS types end up referencing `export =` which confuses NodeNext resolution. We should be consistent: edge build should have `types` that are ESM-style.

alex_h: I found `module.exports` in node_modules/redwood-sdk/dist/cjs/index.cjs. My app bundler didn't tree-shake it away because of a conditional import.

maria_dev: Two practical fixes: A) create a tiny ESM wrapper at the worker root that does `import client from './node_modules/redwood-sdk/dist/esm/index.js'` and then re-export the fetch handler, or B) ask the SDK team to publish an `exports` map with an `import` entry for `./edge` pointing to an ESM build.

kevin-runtime: Wrapper example I use for Workers (module worker):

```
// worker-entry.mjs
import createClient from './dist/esm/client.js';
export default {
  async fetch(request, env) {
    const c = createClient({ key: env.REDWOOD_KEY });
    return c.handle(request);
  }
};
```

kevin-runtime: That avoids any runtime `module.exports` leakage. Also make sure esbuild `platform: 'browser'` and `format: 'esm'` so it doesn't inject CJS wrappers.

sophie-typed: For TS: set `module: 'ESNext'` and `moduleResolution: 'NodeNext'` in tsconfig for dev builds. And when publishing, SDK should include `dist/esm/index.d.ts`.

alex_h: Tried the wrapper, works in Miniflare but CI still failed with the same error on production Workers. The production build pipeline uses rollup instead of esbuild.

maria_dev: Rollup config may include `commonjs()` plugin that converts CJS to ESM but sometimes injects `require`-style helpers. Make sure `output.format: 'esm'` and avoid CommonJS shimming for node-only libs. Also check `inlineDynamicImports` if you use dynamic imports.

kevin-runtime: Another thing: Cloudflare's runtime doesn't have Node globals like `Buffer` or `process`. If the SDK accidentally references Buffer, rollup/esbuild can polyfill that and cause extra CJS helper code. Use `--platform=browser` so polyfills are skipped.

sophie-typed: Small reproducible snippet I posted in the repo issues helps SDK owners triage: a minimal project with just `import { createClient } from 'redwood-sdk'` and a worker-entry that exports fetch. If CI consumes `node`-only code paths you can see `ReferenceError: Buffer is not defined` or `exports is not defined` depending on the transform.

alex_h: Filed a quick GH issue with the repro + CI logs. For now the wrapper + esbuild pipeline trick seems to unblock the feature. Will follow up with PR to add an `exports` edge entry to SDK package.json.

ci-bot: build #5422 — miniflare test passed ✅ — cloudflare deploy failed: ReferenceError: exports is not defined (workers-prod)

maria_dev: Good call opening the issue. SDK team, if you see this thread: an `exports` map for `./edge` and a `dist/esm` build with matching `types` would prevent a bunch of glue wrappers. Also consider publishing a tiny `edge-shim` that only re-exports ESM entrypoints for bundlers that can't statically resolve package exports.

kevin-runtime: If anyone else hits this: quick checklist — 1) confirm bundle is ESM (search for `export` vs `module.exports`), 2) ensure no Node polyfills (Buffer/process), 3) try tiny ESM wrapper as temporary fix, 4) open a small repro issue for SDK owners.

alex_h: Thanks all — wrapper + esbuild `format: 'esm'` fixed it for now. I'll paste the CI config changes in the issue. :thanks:
